# ============================================================================
# ned5_b.hal -- WORKPIECE ROTARY B (joint 6).  Loaded ONLY by -xyzab.
#
# Operator 2026-08-11: "instead of controlling C, everything that used to
# control C now controls the rotaries."  C is still a joint (it gets homed to
# zero and then held there); B is the axis the operator actually drives.
#
# ONE JOINT, TWO CHUCKS.  Each chuck has its OWN worm gear and its OWN stepper
# (operator 2026-08-17).  What couples them is the workpiece held between them,
# nothing else.  They are not a gantry pair -- there is no second feedback to
# square against.  joint.6's single position-cmd feeds both stepgens; the
# scales carry opposite signs, which is the whole of the "twist".
#
#   stepgen.00  7I85S TB2-11/12 STEP+-, TB2-13/14 DIR+-   drive 1   scale +
#   stepgen.01  7I85S TB2-19/20 STEP+-, TB2-21/22 DIR+-   drive 2   scale -
#   (docs/mesa_7i85s_wiring.md:15-16)
#
# SCALE 22.222 steps/deg = 400 p/rev x 20 (worm) / 360  (docs/components.md:50).
# The worm is SELF-LOCKING: B holds its position with the 70 V brick dead, so
# dropping power is safe and costs nothing but the wake-up settle.
#
# POWER.  The brick has two gates in series (operator 2026-08-11): R11, whose
# coil sits on the drive-enable node `*7` and therefore closes whenever the
# machine is enabled (docs/components.md:89), and R4 = 7I84 output-05, which is
# software-driven from brain.r4-select.  R4 is ALSO the PSO pack-select for the
# A/C absolute reads, so B and a head read cannot both have it.  That is
# accepted: a head read only happens while nothing else is moving, B holds
# position unpowered, and the brain arbitrates.  brain.b-armed is the brain's
# statement that R4 has been on long enough for the drives to be live; no step
# may leave the FPGA before it, because an unpowered open-loop drive loses
# position silently.
# ============================================================================

# and2 / timedelay are already loaded with fixed name lists in ned5_iron.hal,
# and a realtime component cannot be loadrt'd twice. `logic` is a different
# component, so the overlay can bring its own instance without editing iron.
# personality 0x102 = 2 inputs, `and` output (logic.comp).
loadrt logic names=b.pwr personality=0x102
addf   b.pwr servo-thread

# --- step/dir config --------------------------------------------------------
# Timings mirror the head stepgens (ned5_iron.hal:685-688) -- UNVERIFIED for
# these drives; they are conservative, not measured.
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.control-type   0
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.step_type      0
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.position-scale [JOINT_6]SCALE
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.maxvel         [JOINT_6]STEPGEN_MAXVEL
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.maxaccel       [JOINT_6]STEPGEN_MAXACCEL
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.steplen        2500
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.stepspace      2500
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.dirsetup       20000
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.dirhold        20000

setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.control-type   0
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.step_type      0
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.position-scale [JOINT_6]SCALE_MIRROR
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.maxvel         [JOINT_6]STEPGEN_MAXVEL
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.maxaccel       [JOINT_6]STEPGEN_MAXACCEL
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.steplen        2500
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.stepspace      2500
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.dirsetup       20000
setp hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.dirhold        20000

# --- command / feedback -----------------------------------------------------
# Open loop: feedback is the stepgen's own commanded position, exactly as the
# head joints do it (ned5_iron.hal:279-282).  Only drive 1 reports; drive 2 is
# the same command through an inverted scale and has nothing extra to say.
# --- SIDE SPLIT (bsplit) ----------------------------------------------------
# Both chucks normally take the same command.  bsplit lets ONE of them be
# jogged while the other stands still, which is how the lash gets preloaded
# out and how the pair gets squared to each other (operator 2026-08-14:
# "selecting LEFT or RIGHT JOGS one or the other ... this lets me preload and
# helps align shit").
#
# BY GATING THE STEPS, NOT BY MOVING THE COMMAND.  cmd-l and cmd-r are ALWAYS
# equal to joint.6.motor-pos-cmd.  The deselected side is stopped at its
# stepgen enable instead.  A disabled hostmot2 stepgen zeroes its step rate
# and ADOPTS position-cmd every cycle (stepgen.c:334-337), so it emits nothing
# while gated and has nothing pending when it returns -- no jump, no slew.
# The drive stays energised throughout: ENA+- is left open, so it never sees
# that pin (docs/mesa_7i85s_wiring.md).
#
# WHY NOT AN OFFSET.  bsplit used to hold the deselected side's OUTPUT still
# and carry the difference in a realtime variable.  Nothing clears a realtime
# variable except unloading the component, so the two chucks stayed commanded
# apart through e-stop, machine-off, R4 and drive power.  On 2026-08-17 that
# reached 22 deg at the joint -- 1.22 turns at the motor -- and with a bar
# clamped in both chucks they twisted against each other and stalled.  Only a
# PB restart cleared it.  Software never needed that memory: each worm is
# self-locking and holds its own chuck with the 70 V brick dead
# (docs/components.md:50).
#
# sel: 1 = LEFT only (RIGHT gated), anything else = BOTH.  Driven from the GUI
# toggle via ned-tab.b-side-out (postgui_b.hal).  Unconnected it reads 0 =
# BOTH, which is the stock behaviour, so a GUI that fails to load cannot leave
# a side gated.
#
# `split` is ACCOUNTING ONLY -- the joint degrees the gated side did not
# receive, netted to ned-tab.b-split-in.  It feeds no command, no feedback and
# no limit, so it cannot fault anything.  There is no bound on it (operator
# 2026-08-16: "delete that compeltely. user will decide how much is too
# much."), and no bound pin exists in bsplit.comp -- a setp for one here would
# ABORT the whole HAL load.
loadrt bsplit
addf   bsplit.0 servo-thread

net b-pos-cmd  joint.6.motor-pos-cmd             => bsplit.0.cmd
net b-cmd-l    bsplit.0.cmd-l                    => hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.position-cmd
net b-cmd-r    bsplit.0.cmd-r                    => hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.position-cmd
# FEEDBACK IS THE LEFT STEPGEN, PASSED THROUGH bsplit.
# Only the RIGHT is ever gated, so stepgen.00 is always live and its
# position-fb always tracks the command.  A real STEPGEN_MAXACCEL/MAXVEL clip
# still shows up as a genuine following error -- the only check an axis with
# no encoder and no drive alarm line has.
net b-fb-l     hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.position-fb => bsplit.0.fb-l
net b-pos-fb   bsplit.0.fb                       => joint.6.motor-pos-fb

# --- enable -----------------------------------------------------------------
# b.pwr = joint.6 wants the amp AND the brain says the brick has settled.
net sig-b-amp-req  joint.6.amp-enable-out => b.pwr.in-00
net sig-b-armed                           => b.pwr.in-01
# The enables come from bsplit, not straight off b.pwr: bsplit ANDs the brick
# state with the side selection, so LEFT-only gates the RIGHT stepgen's steps
# without touching its command.
net sig-b-servo-on b.pwr.and     => bsplit.0.power
net b-en-l         bsplit.0.en-l => hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.00.enable
net b-en-r         bsplit.0.en-r => hm2_7i97.0.stepgen.01.enable
